## Agent CI loop (GitLab)

Agents working in this repo iterate against GitLab CI with `agent-ci`
(from the `gitlab-ci-loop` Claude skill; config in `.agent-ci.yml`).

**Loop**
1. `agent-ci start <topic>` — work on `{{PREFIX}}<topic>`, cut from `{{INTEGRATION}}`.
2. Edit, then `agent-ci check` until local checks pass.
3. Commit (clear, conventional messages), then `agent-ci cycle`:
   local checks → push → MR into `{{INTEGRATION}}` → watch pipeline → print failed job logs.
4. Red? Fix the root cause from the printed logs, commit, `agent-ci cycle` again.
   Stop after {{MAX_ROUNDS}} red rounds (exit code 5) and summarize for the user.
5. Green? Run `agent-ci finish -m "<summary>"` in the background: it squashes, sets the final MR title,
   then waits (read-only) for the user to merge and runs the post-merge diagnosis automatically.
   Report the MR URL right away; report the post-merge result when the command completes.
6. After the merge, the target can still fail (fanout stages run only there). `finish` runs
   `agent-ci postmerge` for you (or run `agent-ci postmerge --mr <iid>` / `agent-ci follow --mr <iid>`;
   read-only): it follows fanout pipelines named in job logs and groups failures by root cause. Check the
   attempt log, then fix with `agent-ci start <topic> --base staging` (if allowed in `mr_targets`), and log what
   you tried with `agent-ci postmerge --note`. Exit code 5 means stop and report. Infra/IAM failures: read-only
   GCP diagnosis only; never apply.

**Guardrails**
- Never push to {{PROTECTED}}; `agent-ci` refuses anyway. Never force-push outside `{{PREFIX}}*`.
- Never trigger deploy/apply/rollout/onboard/offboard pipelines or run `terraform apply`
  unless the user explicitly asks. Dry-runs and plans are fine.
- Never commit secrets, tokens or `.agent-ci/` (local cache).
- Prefer fixing code over weakening checks; don't edit CI rules just to get green.
